🧩 Step 5: What is LSA (Most Important Concept 🔥)

Now we learn the heart of OSPF

🧠 What is LSA?
👉 LSA = Link State Advertisement
It means:
➡️ A router advertises (tells):
Who it is
Which networks it connects to
What neighbors it has

🔥 Simple Explanation
Imagine 3 routers:
R1
R2
R3
What happens:
R1 sends LSA:
👉 “I am connected to R2”
R2 sends LSA:
👉 “I am connected to R1 and R3”
👉 All routers collect this info and build a full network map

🧠 Key Idea (VERY IMPORTANT)
👉 OSPF does NOT share routes directly
👉 It shares LSAs (topology information)

🔁 LSA Flooding
When one router gets LSA
It sends to all neighbors

👉 This is called flooding

📦 Think Like This

👉 LSA = network update message

🔥 Interview Gold Line

👉 “OSPF uses LSAs to build a topology database and calculate shortest path”

🧠 Key Idea (VERY IMPORTANT)

👉 OSPF does NOT share routes directly
👉 It shares LSAs (topology information)

==========================================================================================
🧩 Step 6: Types of LSA (Interview Favorite 🔥)
We’ll keep it very simple — only 3 types first

🧠 Why different LSA types?

👉 Not all information is the same
So OSPF uses different LSA types for different purposes

🔥 3 Important LSA Types
1️⃣ Type 1 – Router LSA

👉 Created by every router

It contains:
Its own links
Its neighbors

👉 “This is MY network info”

🖼️ Router LSA

2️⃣ Type 2 – Network LSA

👉 Created by DR (Designated Router)

It contains:
All routers in that network

👉 “These routers are connected here”

🖼️ Network LSA

3️⃣ Type 3 – Summary LSA

👉 Created by ABR (Area Border Router)

It contains:
Routes from one area to another

👉 “This network exists in another area”


🖼️ Summary LSA
🧠 Easy Memory Trick
Type 1 → Router
Type 2 → DR
Type 3 → Area

==================================================================================
🧩 Step 7: DR & BDR (Very Important 🔥)
Now we understand something very practical in real networks

🧠 Problem First

Imagine 5 routers in same network:

👉 If all talk to each other → too much traffic 😵

🖼️ Without DR (Mess)

✅ Solution: DR & BDR
👉 OSPF selects:
DR (Designated Router) → Main
BDR (Backup Designated Router) → Backup

With DR (Clean)
🧠 How it works
All routers talk only to DR
DR shares updates to everyone
👉 Reduces traffic 

🔥 Real Example
Routers: R1, R2, R3, R4
R2 becomes DR
R3 becomes BDR
👉 Others talk only to R2

⚙️ How DR is Selected
1️⃣ Highest Priority wins
(Default = 1)

2️⃣ If tie → Highest Router ID wins

📌 Important
👉 DR/BDR happens only in:

Broadcast networks (like Ethernet)
👉 NOT in:
Point-to-point links
