🧩 Step 8: OSPF Cost (How “Shortest Path” is Calculated)
Now we answer the big question:

👉 How does OSPF decide which path is best?

🧠 Key Idea

👉 OSPF does NOT count hops like RIP

👉 It uses something called COST

📌 What is Cost?

👉 Cost = “How expensive is this path?”

Lower cost → Better path ✅
Higher cost → Worse path ❌
🔢 Formula (Don’t worry, simple)

Cost=Interface Bandwidth/Reference Bandwidth
	​
🧠 Simple Understanding
Fast link (like 1 Gbps) → Low cost
Slow link (like 10 Mbps) → High cost

👉 OSPF prefers faster links
🔥 Example

Two paths to reach network:

Path 1 → Cost = 10
Path 2 → Cost = 5

👉 OSPF chooses Path 2 ✅
📌 Important Interview Point
👉 Default reference bandwidth = 100 Mbps
⚠️ This is important because:
Modern networks (1G, 10G) need tuning
🧠 Easy Memory Trick
👉 Cost ↓ = Better
==============================================================================

🧩 Step 9: OSPF Areas (Super Important 🔥)

This is where many people get confused… I’ll make it very simple

🧠 Problem First

Imagine a very large network with 100+ routers:

👉 Every router stores full network info → heavy CPU 😵

✅ Solution: Areas

👉 OSPF divides network into smaller parts called Areas

What is an Area?

👉 Area = group of routers
🔥 Most Important Rule
👉 All areas must connect to:
⭐ Area 0
👉 This is called the backbone area

🧠 Simple Example
Area 0 → Backbone
Area 1 → Office network
Area 2 → Data center

👉 Area 1 & 2 must connect to Area 0

📌 Special Router

👉 ABR (Area Border Router)

Connects two areas
Shares information between them
🧠 Why Areas?
Reduce CPU usage
Reduce memory
Faster performance
🔥 Easy Memory Trick

👉 “All roads lead to Area 0”

