🟢 LSA Type 1 — Router LSA
📌 What is it?

👉 Type 1 LSA is created by every router

It tells: “This is my network, these are my links”

📦 What information does it contain?
Router ID
All its interfaces
Link types (point-to-point, etc.)
Cost of each link
Neighbor relationships
📍 Where does it go?

👉 ONLY inside its own area

It is NOT shared to other areas
Each area has its own Type 1 LSAs
🧠 Why is it important?

👉 This is the foundation of OSPF

Used to build LSDB (topology map)
Without it → no routing calculation

***********************************************************************************
🔵 LSA Type 2 — Network LSA
📌 What is it?
👉 Type 2 LSA is created by the DR (Designated Router)
It tells: “These routers are connected in this network”

📦 What information does it contain?
List of all routers (Router IDs) in that network
DR’s own Router ID

👉 It basically shows:

“Who are all connected here”

📍 Where is it used?

👉 Only in:

Broadcast networks (like Ethernet LAN)
Multi-access networks

❌ Not used in point-to-point links

🧠 Why do we need it?

Without Type 2 ❌:

Every router forms adjacency with every other router
Too many connections (messy)

With Type 2 ✅:

DR manages communication
Reduces complexity
🔁 Real Example

Imagine 4 routers in same LAN:

R1, R2, R3, R4
R1 becomes DR

👉 R1 creates Type 2 LSA:

“R1, R2, R3, R4 are connected in this network”

🏢 Simple Analogy

Classroom example:

Students = Routers
Class leader = DR

👉 Leader says:

“These are all students in this class”

⚡ Key Points
Generated by DR only
Contains list of routers in network
Used in broadcast networks
Helps reduce adjacency complexity
⚡ One-Line Answer

**********************************************************************
🟡 LSA Type 3 — Summary LSA
📌 What is it?

👉 Type 3 LSA is created by the ABR (Area Border Router)

It tells: “Here are routes from my area to other areas”

📦 What information does it contain?
Network prefixes (routes)
Cost to reach those networks

👉 It does NOT send full topology, only summary routes

📍 Where does it go?

👉 From one area ➝ to another area (via Area 0)

Used for inter-area communication
🧠 Why do we need it?

Without Type 3 ❌:

Every area would need full topology of all other areas
Huge memory + slow performance

With Type 3 ✅:

Only summary info shared
Efficient & scalable
🔁 Real Example
Area 1 has networks:
10.1.1.0
10.1.2.0

👉 ABR sends to Area 0:

“To reach Area 1, use these routes”

👉 That info is Type 3 LSA

🏢 Simple Analogy

Different departments in a company:

You don’t share full employee details

You just say:

“For HR, contact this extension”

👉 That’s summary info = Type 3

⚡ Key Points
Generated by ABR
Used for between areas
Contains summary routes (not full topology)
Reduces load and improves scalability